course: biology
lesson: 340
title: Weeds and wildflowers: dandelions, nettles, daisies, clover and plantain
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A weed is not a kind of plant; it is a plant growing where somebody does not want it. The same dandelion is a weed on Dad's lawn and a feast for the first bees of spring.
explain: Run through the long grass in shorts and the nettles bite back: their hairs are tiny brittle needles that snap and push stinging chemicals into your skin. Then you spot black spiky caterpillars on those same nettles, the babies of peacock and red admiral butterflies.
explain: Children play with wildflowers everywhere: blowing a dandelion clock to «tell the time», holding a buttercup under a chin to see who likes butter, firing plantain heads like little guns, and hunting for a lucky four-leaf clover.
explain: Most of these games prove nothing, but the plants behind them are clever: parachute seeds, shiny petals, stings for defence, and roots that make their own fertiliser from the air.
fact: Each dandelion seed hangs from a tiny parachute, a dandelion clock holds about 150 to 200 seeds, and blowing it does not tell the time.
fact: The name dandelion comes from French words meaning «lion's tooth», because the leaves have jagged teeth, and dandelion flowers close at night and in rain.
fact: Nettle hairs are tiny brittle needles that break and push chemicals into the skin; the sting fades in a few hours, and rubbing with dock leaves may help a little, but there is little proof.
fact: Peacock and red admiral caterpillars eat nettles, so a nettle patch helps butterflies, and young nettles can be cooked into soup and lose their sting.
fact: A daisy is not one flower but hundreds of tiny flowers packed together; its name comes from «day's eye» because it opens in the day, and it grows low so mowers miss it.
fact: Clover leaves usually have three leaflets, and four-leaf clovers are rare, about one in many thousands, though some people find many by looking hard.
fact: Clover roots have tiny lumps full of helpful bacteria that make natural fertiliser from the air, so farmers grow clover to feed the soil.
fact: Ribwort plantain stems can be looped to flick their heads like little guns, and in Ukraine people put plantain leaves on cuts, but a cut is best cleaned with water and covered with a plaster.
fact: A weed is just a plant growing where people do not want it; many weeds feed bees and other insects, and dandelions are among the first food for bees in spring.
fact: A buttercup held under the chin makes a yellow glow because a shiny layer in its petals reflects light; everyone's chin glows, so it proves nothing about liking butter.
exercise: What carries a dandelion seed through the air? | A tiny parachute | A pair of wings | A sticky hook | A bird's beak only
exercise: About how many seeds are in one dandelion clock? | About 150 to 200 | About 5 | About 20,000 | Exactly 12, like a clock
exercise: Does blowing a dandelion clock tell you the time? | No, it is just a game | Yes, one puff for each hour | Yes, but only in the morning | Only if no seeds are left
exercise: What does the name dandelion mean? | Lion's tooth | Yellow sunshine | Bee's bread | Golden star
exercise: When do dandelion flowers close? | At night and in rain | At midday in bright sun | Only when bees land | Never; they stay open until they die
exercise: How does a nettle sting? | Brittle hairs snap and push chemicals into the skin | It bites with tiny teeth that grow along the edges of its leaves | It sprays poison from its roots | Its leaves are red-hot
exercise: Do dock leaves cure nettle stings? | They may help a little, but there is little proof | Yes, every single time, and the sting is gone within a second | No, they make the sting much worse | Only if you eat the dock leaf
exercise: Which caterpillars eat nettles? | Peacock and red admiral caterpillars | Ladybird larvae and bumblebee grubs | Bumblebee grubs | Dragonfly nymphs
exercise: What happens to young nettles cooked into soup? | They lose their sting | They sting even more | They turn into dock leaves | They become poisonous
exercise: What is a daisy really? | Hundreds of tiny flowers packed together | One flower with a hundred petals | A white leaf with a yellow seed | A mushroom shaped like a flower
exercise: Where does the name daisy come from? | «Day's eye», because it opens in the day | «Dairy», because cows like to eat it in the fields | «Lazy», because it grows low | «Dice», because of its shape
exercise: How many leaflets does a clover leaf usually have? | Three | Seven | Five | Two
exercise: How rare is a four-leaf clover? | About one in many thousands | One in every three | One in every patch | Only one in the whole world
exercise: What lives in the tiny lumps on clover roots? | Helpful bacteria that make fertiliser from the air | Clover seeds waiting under the ground to sprout next spring | Baby worms | Ant eggs
exercise: Why do farmers grow clover in a field? | To feed the soil | To keep cows away | To scare off birds | To grow four-leaf clovers to sell
exercise: What is the best care for a small cut? | Clean it with water and cover it with a plaster | Press a plantain leaf on it, tie it on with grass and forget it | Rub it with a nettle | Leave it dirty to heal by itself
exercise: What is a weed? | A plant growing where people do not want it | A special family of plants that are all poisonous to eat | Any plant with yellow flowers | A plant with no roots
exercise: Why does a buttercup make your chin glow yellow? | A shiny layer in its petals reflects light | Its yellow pollen rubs off and paints your chin | It shows that you like butter | Your chin glows in the sun by itself
test: «ha! chin's yellow, you love butter, admit it» What is true? | Everyone's chin glows; the shiny petals reflect light | Only people who love butter glow, so it really is a good test | Only butter lovers glow | It glows only on boys
test: «dad says dandelions are weeds, kill 'em all» Is a weed a kind of plant or an idea? | An idea: a weed is a plant where people do not want it | A kind of plant: weeds are always poisonous and only grow on lawns | A kind of plant, always poisonous | A kind of plant with no flowers
test: What would happen to early spring bees if every dandelion were sprayed? | They would lose one of their first foods | They would get more food | Nothing, bees never visit dandelions | They would eat the grass instead
test: «nettles are useless, rip 'em all out» What would a butterfly fan say? | Leave some; peacock and red admiral caterpillars need them | Rip them out, because nettle leaves poison every kind of butterfly | Nettles poison all butterflies | Butterflies only eat roses
test: Why would a plant need stings? | To stop animals eating it | To catch flies for food | To keep warm in winter | To help its seeds fly
test: In a cartoon a girl blows a dandelion clock and says «three o'clock!». What is right? | The puffs do not tell the time; the seeds just fly off on parachutes | She is right: each puff is one hour, so it works in summer when the seeds are ripe | Each seed is one minute | Dandelions keep exact time
test: «nan put a plantain leaf on my cut knee, that's proper medicine innit» What is best? | Clean the cut with water and cover it with a plaster | A leaf is better than any plaster, so leave it on for a week | Rub dirt in to stop the blood | Leave the leaf on for a week
test: Why do daisies survive a lawnmower? | They grow low, so the blades pass over them | Their stems are made of metal | They pull themselves underground | Mowers cannot cut white flowers
test: Why are four-leaf clovers rare? | Clover nearly always grows three leaflets, and a fourth is an unusual change | Four-leaf clovers grow only high on mountains, where cows eat most of them first | Bees nibble the fourth leaf off most of them | They only grow on 29 February
test: Why would a flower close at night? | To protect its pollen from cold and damp when bees are not flying | To sleep through the dark hours, like a person, and wake up fresh | To sleep like a person | To hide from the moon
test: Count them: 3 rabbits nibble the clover and a fox watches from the hedge. How many ears and how many tails? | 8 ears and 4 tails | 6 ears and 4 tails | 8 ears and 3 tails | 4 ears and 4 tails
test: A farmer's field has no fertiliser but a lot of clover, and the next crop grows well. What explains it? | Bacteria in clover root lumps made fertiliser from the air | Clover leaves fell and turned into soil overnight | Bees brought fertiliser from the town | Rabbits watered the field
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term «weed»? | It denotes any plant growing in a location where its presence is not desired | It denotes a distinct botanical family of poisonous plants | It denotes exclusively plants bearing yellow flowers | It denotes plants that are incapable of producing seed
exam: Identify the mechanism by which the common nettle inflicts its sting. | Brittle hairs fracture upon contact and introduce irritant chemicals into the skin | The leaves emit a heated vapour upon contact | The roots discharge a venom into the surrounding soil | Small teeth upon the stem lacerate the skin
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the cultivation of clover by farmers. | Bacteria within nodules upon the roots of clover produce fertiliser from the air, enriching the soil | Clover is cultivated solely for the purpose of obtaining four-leaf specimens, which command a high price at market | Clover deprives the soil of all nourishment | Clover repels all insects from the field
exam: Ascertain the cause of the yellow reflection produced when a buttercup is held beneath the chin. | A glossy layer within the petals reflects light, irrespective of any preference of the subject | The subject's fondness for butter is thereby demonstrated | Pollen is deposited upon the skin of the subject | The skin of the subject emits a yellow light in sunshine
